Do not build with -march=native

Both Makefiles add -march=native, which tunes the library and the two tools
to the CPU of the machine that builds the package; on an older CPU the
result can die with an illegal instruction. The vectorised code path does
not depend on it: -DENABLE_SSE2 selects it, and it uses only SSE
intrinsics.

Arch Linux deletes the same two lines with sed in its PKGBUILD.

Upstream: not submitted (the Makefiles say "Modify as required")
Applies-to: all environments

diff --git a/apps/Makefile b/apps/Makefile
index 4705903..a7025ce 100644
--- a/apps/Makefile
+++ b/apps/Makefile
@@ -25,7 +25,6 @@ MANDIR ?= /usr/share/man/man1
 VERSION = 1.10.1
 CPPFLAGS += -MMD -MP -DVERSION=\"$(VERSION)\"
 CXXFLAGS += -O2  -ffast-math -Wall
-CXXFLAGS += -march=native
 
 
 all:	zresample zretune zresample.1.gz zretune.1.gz
diff --git a/source/Makefile b/source/Makefile
index f9f1d88..e2ba29d 100644
--- a/source/Makefile
+++ b/source/Makefile
@@ -34,7 +34,6 @@ CPPFLAGS += -I. -D_REENTRANT -D_POSIX_PTHREAD_SEMANTICS
 CPPFLAGS += -DENABLE_SSE2
 #CPPFLAGS += -DENABLE_NEON  
 CXXFLAGS += -Wall -fPIC -O2 -ffast-math
-CXXFLAGS += -march=native
 LDFLAGS += 
 LDLIBS +=
 
